      "No Stoic treatise on logic survives. Chrysippus wrote, by the catalogue at Diogenes Laertius 7.189 to 202, well over a hundred books on logical subjects, including two on redundant arguments (7.195) and eight on the Nobody puzzle (7.198); of these we have titles, one papyrus (the Logical Investigations, PHerc. 307), and nothing else. What we call Stoic logic is assembled from a doxographical digest (DL 7.49 to 83, drawn, by Diogenes' own statement at 7.48, from the Epitome of Diocles of Magnesia), from two Pyrrhonist attacks (Sextus, PH 2 and M 8), from a physician who learned the system and wrote his own (Galen), from two Peripatetic commentators who restate Stoic rules in Aristotelian terms before rejecting them (Alexander, Simplicius), from a Latin handbook of doubted authorship (Apuleius), from the earliest but most partisan witness (Cicero), and from a Christian apologist quoting a Stoic theorem for his own ends (Origen). Each has a reason to report and a reason to distort, and the difficulty is that the two are correlated: the fullest sources are the hostile ones. The session's problem is methodological. One cannot discount a witness without a theory of what it distorts, and the theory has to be built from the witnesses. The exercise applies the corpus admission tests (ACQUISITION_PLAN Part 4) to two Themata ledger items, S001 and S018, to see how far a standard written for incoming texts can be made to grade evidence.",
