          "The most consequential evidential choice in the modern literature concerns which wording of the third thema to trust, because the reconstruction of all four themata is built outward from it. Mates (Stoic Logic, 1953), Kneale (The Development of Logic, 1962), Becker, Mueller and Mignucci all begin from Alexander's formula at in An. Pr. 278.12 to 14: when from two a third follows, and from the third and an external premiss another follows, then that other follows from the first two and the external premiss. Read this way the third thema is a form of cut, and the natural reconstruction of the themata is a set of chain rules. To obtain completeness on this basis Mueller and Mignucci add structural rules (reflexivity, monotonicity, contraction) and connective rules (and-elimination, or-introduction) that are not attested.",
          "Bobzien (1996, pp. 145 to 151) argues that Alexander's wording is shaped by the Aristotelian passage he is commenting on, that it is stated from the point of view of synthesis (building up a longer argument), whereas the Stoic use of the themata was analysis (breaking down a given argument into indemonstrables), and that Simplicius's differently worded version (in De Caelo 237.2 to 4) is nearer the Stoic original. Her T3 is then a chain-inference rule applied backwards, with the requirement that each application split off one two-premiss argument. The added structural rules she rejects on the ground that the Stoics explicitly denied the corresponding properties: an argument with a redundant premiss is invalid (M 8.431, PH 2.146 to 150), so monotonicity cannot be assumed; 'p, therefore p' is not an argument (plurality of premisses), so reflexivity cannot be assumed.",
