    briefing:
      "The Stoic syllogistic is a logic of assertibles (ἀξιώματα, axiōmata), not of sentences and not of propositions in the modern sense. An assertible is a complete sayable (λεκτόν, lekton) that can be stated, and it is true or false at a time: 'it is day' is true now and will be false tonight, and it is the same assertible on both occasions (DL 7.65 to 66). That single feature separates the Stoic theory from Frege's and from most of what the modern student has been taught to mean by 'proposition'. It also generates the hard cases that this session turns on: a definite assertible, 'this one is dead', is said to be destroyed when the person it points at dies (Alexander in An. Pr. 177.25 to 178.4), so there are assertibles that cannot be true, not because they are false but because they cannot be stated; and a negation is formed by prefixing a negative particle to a whole assertible, with the negation of a negation, the hyper-negation (ὑπεραποφατικόν), said to 'posit' the original (DL 7.69). The second claim is the one the Themata project found it could not do without: under any reading of the contradictory of 'not p' other than p, the Sorites chain (S018) is underivable. The session's difficulty is that the texts on assertibles are brief, that the example sentence for the hyper-negation at DL 7.69 rests on an editorial supplement, and that the two fullest witnesses, Diogenes and Sextus, do not divide simple assertibles in quite the same way.",
