    briefing:
      "Cicero reports (Academica 2.143) that on the question how to judge whether a conditional such as 'if it is day, it is light' is true, Diodorus held one view, Philo another, and Chrysippus a third, and that the quarrel was notorious. Sextus (PH 2.110 to 112) gives four criteria: Philo's, on which a conditional is false only when its antecedent is true and its consequent false; Diodorus's, on which it is true when it neither could nor can begin with a truth and end in a falsehood; the criterion of connection (συνάρτησις, synartēsis), on which it is true when the contradictory of its consequent conflicts with its antecedent; and a criterion of 'emphasis', on which the consequent must be potentially contained in the antecedent. Diogenes 7.73 gives the connection criterion as the Stoic one. The session's difficulty is threefold. First, the connection criterion uses 'conflict' (μάχη, machē), and no surviving text defines it; second, the same relation reappears in the Stoic criterion of validity (PH 2.137), so that validity is defined through the conditional and the conditional through conflict, and whether that is a circle or a single primitive is disputed; third, the other connectives are not truth-functional either: the Stoic disjunction is exclusive, announcing that exactly one disjunct is true (DL 7.72, Gellius 16.8.13 to 14), and Chrysippus advised the diviners to state their theorems as negated conjunctions rather than conditionals (Cicero, De Fato 12 to 15), which shows the conditional was stronger than material implication and the negated conjunction weaker. The Themata project encodes the conditional reading as a parameter for exactly this reason.",
