          "Diogenes Laertius 7.73 (Hicks, Loeb 1925): ἀληθὲς δ' ἐστὶ συνημμένον οὗ τὸ ἀντικείμενον τοῦ λήγοντος μάχεται τῷ ἡγουμένῳ, οἷον 'εἰ ἡμέρα ἐστί, φῶς ἐστι'. τοῦτ' ἀληθές ἐστι· τὸ γὰρ 'οὐχὶ φῶς', ἀντικείμενον τῷ λήγοντι, μάχεται τῷ 'ἡμέρα ἐστί'.",
          "Transliteration: alēthes d' esti synēmmenon hou to antikeimenon tou lēgontos machetai tōi hēgoumenōi, hoion 'ei hēmera esti, phōs esti'. tout' alēthes esti; to gar 'ouchi phōs', antikeimenon tōi lēgonti, machetai tōi 'hēmera esti'. Hicks: 'A true hypothetical proposition is one the opposite of whose consequent is contradictory to its antecedent, e.g. If it is day, it is light. This is true, for the statement It is not light, which is the opposite of the consequent, contradicts It is day.'",
          "Gloss. συνημμένον (synēmmenon): the conditional, literally 'what has been joined together', from συνάπτω; the related noun συνάρτησις (synartēsis, 'connection') names the criterion at PH 2.111. ἡγούμενον (hēgoumenon): the antecedent, 'what leads'; the same word names the leading premiss of an indemonstrable (session 6). λῆγον (lēgon): the consequent, 'what ends'. ἀντικείμενον (antikeimenon): the contradictory, formed by prefixing οὐχί (session 2); here τὸ 'οὐχὶ φῶς' is given as the contradictory of 'φῶς ἐστι', with the verb dropped. μάχεται (machetai): 'fights with', 'conflicts with'; a word from combat, not from the logical vocabulary.",
          "The construal that matters. Hicks renders μάχεται as 'contradicts', which is wrong in a way that decides the question. 'Not: it is light' does not contradict 'it is day' in the Stoic sense, since the contradictory of 'it is day' is 'not: it is day' and nothing else. What is asserted is that the two conflict, and the whole content of the criterion lies in what conflict is. Three options. (i) Logical incompatibility: the two cannot both be true as a matter of form; then almost no ordinary conditional is true, since 'it is day' and 'not: it is light' are formally compatible, and the Stoics' own example fails. (ii) Incompatibility given the natures of things, physical or conceptual: day and darkness cannot coexist because of what day is; then the criterion is modal and non-formal, and the truth of a conditional depends on knowledge of the world, which fits the diviners' passage (Cicero, Fat. 12 to 15), where the connection between a birth under the Dog Star and death at sea is denied because no such nature links them. (iii) Incompatibility in the sense that the one excludes the other at every time, which collapses the criterion into Diodorus's. The texts support (ii): the examples are drawn from natures (day and light, walking and moving), and PH 2.111 rejects 'if it is day, Dion is walking' by appeal to the absence of conflict, not of co-occurrence. But (ii) is an interpretation, and the student who adopts it must say that the ancient texts give examples, not a definition. The Themata parameter 'conditional reading' (Philonian, Diodorean, Chrysippean, containment) encodes the alternatives and cannot encode (ii) without a model of natures; what it encodes as 'Chrysippean' should be stated by the student in a sentence.",
