        answer:
          "Objection (the position Mates, Stoic Logic 1953, ch. 3, is usually read as holding: that the Stoic logic of the indemonstrables works with a material conditional and that the connection criterion is a separate theory of truth): modus ponens and modus tollens are valid under material implication; the third, fourth and fifth indemonstrables need only exclusive disjunction and negated conjunction, which are truth-functional; so the five forms are classical schemata and the dispute about synartēsis does not touch the syllogistic. Reply (Bobzien 1996 section 5, pp. 181 to 188; CHHP pp. 104 to 107, 121 to 125; Frede 1974 for the prior claim that the Chrysippean conditional is the one the logic uses): (1) validity is defined through the truth of a conditional (PH 2.137), and if that conditional is Chrysippean then validity is not classical consequence, which is confirmed by the rejection of arguments with redundant premisses (M 8.431; PH 2.146 to 150) and of one-premiss arguments (M 8.443), both classically valid; (2) the system lacks and-elimination, or-introduction and if-introduction, all classically available, and has no weakening, so its set of theorems is not the classical set regardless of how the five forms look in isolation; (3) the indemonstrables are evident, for the Stoics, because of the connection the leading premiss asserts, so reading them with a material conditional changes the account of why they need no demonstration. A fair verdict: the five forms are classically valid, so the objector is right that the forms alone do not decide; the structural properties and the validity criterion do, and they are non-classical. The Themata encoding treats the conditional reading as a parameter for exactly this reason, and the student should say which reading the fitting candidates used.",
