          "Derivation. Show that 'if p, q; if q, r; therefore if p, r' (the wholly hypothetical argument) is valid by the Stoic criterion of PH 2.137 under the connection reading, and then explain, citing Bobzien 1996 section 6, why it is nonetheless not a syllogism.",
        answer:
          "Validity by PH 2.137: form the conditional whose antecedent is the conjunction of the premisses and whose consequent is the conclusion: 'if (if p, q, and if q, r), then if p, r'. Under the connection criterion (DL 7.73) this is true when the contradictory of 'if p, r' conflicts with the conjunction 'if p, q, and if q, r'. The contradictory of 'if p, r' is 'not: if p, r', which on the connection reading says that 'not r' does not conflict with p. But if 'not q' conflicts with p and 'not r' conflicts with q, then 'not r' conflicts with p (conflict transmitted along the chain: if p held, q would have to hold, so r would have to hold). So 'not: if p, r' conflicts with the premisses, and the argument is valid. (Note that this step assumes conflict is transitive in this way, which the texts do not state; say so.) Not a syllogism: Bobzien 1996 section 6 holds that a syllogism is either an indemonstrable or reducible to indemonstrables by the themata (DL 7.78; PH 2.156 to 157), that every indemonstrable has a simple assertible or the contradictory of one among its premisses or conclusion as the co-assumption or the conclusion, and that the wholly hypothetical argument has no such component: its premisses and conclusion are all conditionals, no indemonstrable matches any part of it, and the themata only split arguments into two-premiss pieces that are themselves indemonstrables. Hence it is valid in the generic sense (DL 7.78's 'conclusive') and not in the specific sense; it belongs with the subsyllogistic and unmethodically concluding arguments of session 11 (Alexander in An. Pr. 84, 345, 373; Galen Inst. Log. 17 to 19).",
