          "Derivation. Formalise the three definitions of the possible (Philo, Diodorus, Chrysippus) over a discrete time line and prove that Diodorean possibility implies Chrysippean possibility, stating the assumption the proof needs; then give a countermodel to the converse.",
        answer:
          "Set-up: times t0 < t1 < ...; now = tn; H a set of admissible courses of events with the actual course h* in H; A true or false at each time in each course; Hind(A, t) a predicate for external hindrance. Poss_Ph(A) iff there exist h in H and t with A true at t in h. Poss_D(A) iff there exists t ≥ tn with A true at t in h*. Poss_C(A) iff Poss_Ph(A) and there exists t ≥ tn with not Hind(A, t). Assumption needed: (Hind) if A is true at t in h*, then not Hind(A, t); that is, nothing actually true was actually hindered. Proof: suppose Poss_D(A). Then A is true at some t ≥ tn in h*. Since h* is in H, Poss_Ph(A). By (Hind), not Hind(A, t) for that t ≥ tn. Hence Poss_C(A). Countermodel to Poss_C implying Poss_D: let A = 'this jewel is broken'; let the jewel be breakable (some h in H has A true at some t, so Poss_Ph(A)); let nothing hinder its breaking at any t ≥ tn (not Hind(A, t)); but in h* it is never broken. Then Poss_C(A) and not Poss_D(A). This is the Chrysippean commitment to the third proposition of the Master Argument (Epictetus 2.19.2 to 5), that something is possible which neither is nor will be true; its Diodorean denial is at Cicero Fat. 13 and Boethius in Int. 234 to 235. Sources for the definitions: DL 7.75 (Chrysippus, unnamed), Boethius 234 to 235 (all three), Alexander in An. Pr. 184 (Philo's chaff at the bottom of the ocean). The Chrysippean definition is Bobzien's (C/M) (1993, p. 80); her Scheme 2 (p. 83) shows the same inclusions.",
