          "Premisses: (P1) every past truth is necessary (for past-tensed assertibles: if it was the case that q, then it is necessary that it was the case that q). (P2) if p is possible and q follows from p, then q is possible. (P3, to be refuted) some A is possible and A neither is nor will be true. Added: (A1) if p, then it was always the case that p was going to be; (A2) the rule form of P2. Derivation: suppose A is possible and A neither is nor will be true. From 'A neither is nor will be true' by (A1), it was always the case that A was never going to be true; so at some past time it was the case that A would never be true. By (P1) that past truth is necessary: necessarily, it was the case that A would never be true. From 'A is true' it follows that it was not the case that A would never be true (A's being true now or later contradicts its never being true); so from the possible A follows the contradictory of a necessary truth, which is impossible. By (P2) the impossible has followed from the possible, contradiction. Hence no A satisfies P3: the possible is what is or will be true. Cleanthes stops at P1, denying that every past truth is necessary (Epictetus 2.19.2 to 4). Chrysippus stops at P2, denying that the impossible cannot follow from the possible, with the Dion conditional (Epictetus 2.19.5; Alexander in An. Pr. 177 to 178). A critic of Prior stops at A1, which is a principle of the fixity of the future not attested in Epictetus, and which both Stoics could have rejected without touching the three propositions; Prior's reconstruction (Philosophical Quarterly 1955; Past, Present and Future 1967) therefore shows the three are inconsistent given A1, not that they are inconsistent as the ancients claimed.",
